{"id": "f5f45e26-8d14-40bb-ae9a-964012a70dde", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T08:58:14.394250", "source": "user", "parent_id": "f7b8d9af-65b1-44b6-adb2-608d93ae60a0", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "We are at initial inspection. This is a design-only deliverable (per submission contract): an experiment design with rationale, controls, analysis plan and feasibility — no experimental execution and no fabrication of results. The research question: Does replacing the time attention mechanism in the SiT (Scalable Interpolant Transformers) architecture with a non-causal Hyena operator enable the model to generate extremely long, consistent trajectories that capture dynamics over multiple timescales? Study context is fixed: (a) SiT with original time attention mechanism as one condition, and (b) SiT with non-causal Hyena operator as the contrastive condition. Please perform ONLY the following and report back: (1) Repository/input inspection — list whatever is present under /workspace (file tree at top two levels), identify any SiT-related code, Hyena-related code, configs, READMEs, or notes that constrain or support the design. Flag anything clearly relevant; do not browse deeply. (2) Proposed approach — a concise design outline covering: target task and dataset(s) for short vs. long-horizon trajectory generation; the two model conditions (attention vs. non-causal Hyena) with identical backbone elsewhere; matched compute / parameter count / training schedule controls; sequence-length regimes (short baseline plus a clearly long horizon, e.g. multi-thousand-step rollout); metrics for consistency over long horizons and multi-timescale fidelity (e.g., short-horizon error, long-horizon drift, spectral / autocorrelation agreement per timescale, distributional scores on rollout ensembles); training and evaluation protocol; number of seeds and statistical read-out; compute and run-time feasibility given a CPU-only, no-GPU, no-network bubblewrap environment with 24 vCPUs and system Python (note any need for small-scale or proxy demonstrations if full training is infeasible). (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties — e.g. what dataset/task proxies stand in for 'long, consistent, multi-timescale trajectories' (e.g., chaotic or stiff ODE/SDE systems, video frame prediction, motion-capture, physical simulations), how 'consistency' and 'multi-timescale' are operationalized, what is held fixed across conditions, what evidence would answer the question versus merely correlate with it, and any environment-imposed constraints (no GPU, no network, no preinstalled deps) that force simplifications. Do not produce the final design yet.", "required_handoff_stage": "plan", "approach_approved": false, "public_task": {"question": "Does replacing the time attention mechanism with a non-causal Hyena operator in the SiT architecture allow the model to generate extremely long, consistent trajectories that capture dynamics over multiple timescales?", "context": "Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.", "environment": {"runtime": "bubblewrap", "network": "disabled", "repository": "/workspace", "python": "python3", "software_environment": "system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled", "datasets": [], "gpu_enabled": false, "cpu_affinity_count": 24, "note": "Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment."}, "researcher_topics": ["neurips2024/93022/6:setup_4", "neurips2024/93022/6:setup_5"], "question_budget": 6, "actor_step_budget": 60, "continue_after_initial_plan": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
