/Ti and possibly restricting to Lorenz-96 only; both are reductions that should be flagged in any subsequent report."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-073ccf3c-e18e-4a09-bde4-0af98903c61c", "tool_call": {"id": "call-073ccf3c-e18e-4a09-bde4-0af98903c61c", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"summary": "Final SiT+Hyena experiment design", "stage": "result", "answer": "# Final experiment design \u2014 SiT sequence-mixer swap (attention vs. non-causal Hyena) for long, multi-timescale-consistent trajectory generation\n\nSubmission contract: design-only deliverable. No experiment has been run. No numerical outcome is fabricated. All cost and runtime figures in \u00a710 are projections against published SiT/Hyena hyperparameters, not measurements.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research question and the mechanistic claim it targets\n\n**Question (verbatim, from task.json).** *Does replacing the time attention mechanism with a non-causal Hyena operator in the SiT architecture allow the model to generate extremely long, consistent trajectories that capture dynamics over multiple timescales?*\n\n**Mechanistic claim under test.** The operator swap changes exactly one property of the SiT sequence mixer: the quadratic, softmax-bounded global attention along the temporal axis is replaced by a long, implicitly-parameterised depthwise convolution with elementwise gating, run bidirectionally. The hypothesis is that this single property \u2014 non-causal long-convolutional sequence mixing \u2014 is what enables (i) flatter error growth as the rollout horizon grows and (ii) faithful reproduction of long-tail (slow-timescale) spectral content that attention either smooths over or pays quadratic cost to capture.\n\nThis is a mechanistic claim, not a benchmark claim. The three-condition design below is what allows the claim to be either supported or refuted rather than merely correlated with.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Repository and runtime observations (evidence grounding this design)\n\n- `/workspace` contains exactly one file: `task.json`. Its body repeats the question / context and a support note stating **\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"** No SiT source, no Hyena source, no configs, READMEs, or notes exist locally.\n- Researcher clarification topics `neurips2024/93022/6:setup_4` and `:setup_5` are referenced in the request but their contents were not surfaced to me; the researcher supplied the operative clarifications directly in the approval message, which are binding below.\n- Python 3.12.3, x86_64, 24 vCPUs, ~688 GB free disk, **no GPU**, no network. `numpy`, `scipy`, `torch` are not importable; `pip` is offline; the bubblewrap blocks fetching.\n- Conclusion: a full SiT+Hyena training run is **not executable in the current environment**. The deliverable is the design, the pre-registered metrics, the analysis plan, and projected cost tables. Everything that would normally be a \"training loss curve\" or \"metric value\" is here only a *definition* of what would be measured.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Study context \u2014 the three conditions\n\n| ID | Sequence mixer | Bidirectionality | Role |\n|----|----------------|------------------|------|\n| **C0** | Multi-head self-attention along the time axis (the SiT baseline sequence mixer) | Non-causal (standard SiT) | Primary contrast reference |\n| **C1** | **Non-causal** Hyena (depthwise long convolution with implicit positional filter + elementwise gating + MLP) | **Bidirectional** | Primary intervention |\n| **C2** | Causal Hyena (identical Hyena block plus a causal mask and half-kernel FFT) | **Causal** | Mechanism-isolating ablation: separates \"long-convolution\" from \"non-causality\" |\n\nC0 \u2194 C1 is the *primary contrast* required by the study context (and enclosed in the public task framing). **C2 is now mandatory per the researcher's binding clarification**, because a flat improvement of C1 over C0 alone cannot distinguish whether the gain comes from the long convolutional kernel size or from its bidirectionality \u2014 both are conjectured contributors, and only the C0 / C1 / C2 triple can adjudicate.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Backbone (held identical across all conditions)\n\nWhat is **not** swapped (and must remain identical across C0, C1, C2):\n\n- SiT wrapper: state embedding, time/timestep embedding, class/latent conditioning pathway, residual stream topology, layer normalisation placement, normalisation epsilon, residual scaling coefficients, FFN expansion ratio, dropout schedule.\n- The cross-attention path from the time/timestep embedding into the residual stream (per the researcher's binding clarification #1). **Only the sequence/trajectory-axis attention layer inside each SiT block is swapped.**\n- Patch / temporal-stride scheme (one timestep per token, no patching, so the operand of every mixer is a literal sequence of states).\n- Optimiser (AdamW), learning-rate schedule (cosine with linear warm-up), weight decay, gradient clipping threshold, micro-batch size, gradient-accumulation count, total optimiser steps, random initialisation scheme (matched RNG seeds).\n- FP32 only \u2014 no mixed precision (researcher clarification #7).\n- Data pipeline, normalisation statistics, and dynamical-system integrator coefficients for ground-truth generation.\n\nWhat is **swapped** (only the sequence-mixing operator) is defined in \u00a75.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Operator swap specification (binding)\n\n### 5.1. \"Time attention\" \u2014 what is swapped\n\nPer clarification #1, the swapped layer is the attention layer that mixes information **along the temporal axis** inside each SiT block (the sequence mixer on the residual stream). The attention layer that consumes the time/timestep embedding *as context* is held identical across all conditions and is not the subject of the swap. State this verbatim in any implementation readme.\n\n### 5.2. C0 \u2014 multi-head self-attention (baseline)\n\n- Input: residual stream `x \u2208 R^{B \u00d7 L \u00d7 D}`, where `L` is the number of timesteps (`L_train = 512`, see \u00a77) and `D` is the model width.\n- Operation: standard multi-head self-attention with `H` heads, head dim `d_h = D/H`:\n  - `Q, K, V = x W_Q, x W_K, x W_V`, all with shape `(B, H, L, d_h)`.\n  - `Attn(Q, K, V) = softmax(Q K^T / sqrt(d_h)) V`. No causal mask (SiT is non-autoregressive in its native mode). Output projected by `W_O`.\n- FLOPs: `O(L\u00b2 \u00b7 D)` for the attention logits and `O(L\u00b2 \u00b7 D)` for the value aggregation. Dominant term `O(L\u00b2 D)`.\n\n### 5.3. C1 \u2014 non-causal Hyena (primary intervention)\n\n- Input/output: same `(B, L, D)`.\n- Block structure (per Hyena recipe):\n  1. **Depthwise long convolution** parameterised by an *implicit positional filter*. Implementation: a learned implicit filter `h \u2208 C^{2L}` of length `2L` is constructed such that only the first `L` entries are used by the bidirectional mixing (see bidirectionality below); the convolution is computed in the frequency domain as `IFFT(FFT(x, n=2L) \u2299 FFT(h, n=2L))` with the zero-padded input and an FFT of length `2L` for circular-conv correctness. Filter parameterisation: a small MLP maps positional indices `0..2L-1` to a complex-valued filter; this is the \"implicit\" parameterisation (small parameter count, kernel length equal to twice the sequence length).\n  2. **Elementwise gating**: an input-conditioned gate `g = \u03c3(x W_g + b_g)` multiplies the convolved output pointwise. (Identical to Hyena: `y = (x * conv) \u2299 g + x * (1 \u2212 g)` style, with the exact gating form copied from Hyena's published recipe to avoid drift.) No causal mask.\n  3. **MLP block** (GELU) identical to the C0 FFN in width, expansion, and depth.\n- Bidirectionality (binding): for non-causal Hyena, the implicit filter is *real-valued* (symmetric or unconstrained \u2014 to be decided by an ablation register, see \u00a711), of length `2L`, and applied via a single one-sided FFT against the real/complex-conjugated pair (i.e. both past and future positions contribute at index `i` because the FFT mixes indices `0..2L-1` symmetrically with no causal shift). Equivalently and equivalently permitted: two parallel causal filters `h_fwd`, `h_bwd` each of length `L` (one parameterised by `pos i>0`, the other by \"negated\" index) summed output-wise \u2014 both formulations specify the same operator class; the implementation choice is logged in `operators.yaml` so it is reproducible and the same choice is used for all non-causal Hyena seeds.\n- FLOPs: `O(L \u00b7 log L \u00b7 D)` depthwise-convolution FFT + `O(L \u00b7 D)` for gating + `O(L \u00b7 D\u00b2)` for the MLP. Dominant at long `L` is `O(L \u00b7 log L \u00b7 D)` from the FFT.\n\n### 5.4. C2 \u2014 causal Hyena (ablation)\n\n- Identical Hyena block as C1, but:\n  1. The FFT length is `L` (not `2L`) and the filter is real and **causal** in the spectral sense (lower-triangular causal mask applied to the filter before the inverse FFT, and the convolution is computed against the one-sided past-only filter \u2014 Hyena's original causal formulation).\n  2. An explicit causal mask `M[i,j] = 0 if j>i else 1` is applied to the gate-input mixing (kept simple \u2014 causal mask on the gate, not on the conv, the conv is causally parameterised at construction time).\n- FLOPs: identical to C1 asymptotics `O(L \u00b7 log L \u00b7 D)`. Memory lower than C1 because the kernel is half the length.\n- This condition exists **only** to attribute any C0 \u2192 C1 gain to bidirectionality. If C1 \u2248 C2 > C0 the gain is \"long convolution\"; if C1 > C2 \u2248 C0 the gain is \"bidirectionality on a long kernel\"; if C1 > C2 > C0 it is \"both\". Anything else (e.g. C2 > C1) falsifies the researcher's hypothesis.\n\n### 5.5. Parameter-count matching (binding)\n\nFor each `(depth D_layers, width D, head dim d_h, MLP expansion r)` chosen, the Hyena block has parameters dominated by `W_g` (\u223c `D\u00b2`) and the implicit-filter MLP (small, independent of `L`). C0's attention has `4 D\u00b2` (`W_Q,W_K,W_V,W_O`). The Hyena-implicit filter parameterisation contributes a fixed-size MLP (\u2264 `O(2L)` if positional-index MLP, but with weight sharing across positions it remains O(1) in `L` and O(D) at most).\n\nProcedure: pick a SiT baseline (e.g. SiT-B/2 width/depth type envelope; the public task does not require specifying the exact envelope so long as it is fixed and logged). **Adjust the channel width of the Hyena block by \u2264 5% via a bottleneck re-projection** (`x \u2192 R^{B\u00d7L\u00d7D'} \u2192 Hyena \u2192 R^{B\u00d7L\u00d7D} \u2192 x`). Log `D'/D` for every Hyena seed. Verify total trainable parameter count is within \u00b15% across C0 / C1 / C2 (counting only the mixer; the wrapper is shared). If strict \u00b15% cannot be achieved without distorting Hyena's recipe, log the gap and include a sensitivity sweep (see \u00a711).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Dataset \u2014 primary benchmark\n\nPrimary benchmark: **multi-timescale dynamical systems** with reference ground truth, per researcher clarification #5. The secondary video probe is explicitly dropped.\n\nFour systems, each chosen because ground truth is analytic/numerically exact at any horizon and the multi-timescale structure is well-characterised:\n\n| System | Why it tests multi-timescale behaviour | Horizon / integration |\n|--------|---------------------------------------|------------------------|\n| Lorenz-96 (`F=8`, `N\u226540`) | Coupled slow manifold + fast wave modes; chaotic, Lyapunov time scale well-defined (~T=1) | Octave-stable LSTM/consistency test up to 16384 steps |\n| Robertson stiff chemistry (3-species autocalytic) | Three-species stiffness ratio ~10\u2076; fast-equilibrating + slow-accumulating dynamics | Stiff reference ground truth (LSODA) |\n| Kuramoto\u2013Sivashinsky (1-D, L \u2248 30\u03c0, periodic) | Spatiotemporal chaos with at least three spectral regimes (inverse cascade, equipartition, dissipation roll-off) | Spectral-band ground truth |\n| Van der Pol chain (\u22658 coupled oscillators with detuned \u00b5) | Multiple weakly-coupled limit-cycle oscillators produce beat frequencies + slow envelope dynamics | Slow-envelope ground truth |\n\nGround truth: produced *offline* (not at training time) by a stiff-aware integrator (RK45 adaptive for non-stiff; LSODA for stiff subsystems) at `dt_ref = 1/256` then downsampled to `dt = 1` per token. Trajectories are normalised state-wise to zero-mean unit-variance. Dataset size: train \u2265 1M token-step pairs (e.g. \u2265 2000 trajectories \u00d7 512 steps, with random initial conditions per system), validation 50 trajectories \u00d7 16384, test 50 \u00d7 16384 (held out).\n\nWhy synthetic-dynamical and not video/mocap: (i) multi-timescale structure is analytically characterised (autocorrelation, PSD, Lyapunov exponent, stiffness ratio), (ii) ground truth is available at *any* horizon including 16384, (iii) determinism + reproducibility across seeds.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Sequence-length regimes (pre-registered)\n\n| Phase | Length | Purpose |\n|-------|--------|---------|\n| **Training snippet** `L_train = 512` | 512 | Fixed, equal budget across conditions |\n| **Short baseline** | 128, 256 | In-distribution evaluation |\n| **Mid horizon** | 1024 | First out-of-distribution stress |\n| **Long horizon** | 4096, 8192, **16384** | The \"extremely long\" regime |\n\nPre-registration means the *exact* set `{128, 256, 1024, 4096, 8192, 16384}` is fixed before any rollout is examined; no horizon is added or removed after seeing results.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Matched-compute controls (binding)\n\n- **Identical total training FLOPs budget** across C0, C1, C2. The total is computed analytically as `T = N_steps \u00b7 B \u00b7 (F_wrapper + F_mixer(C))` where `F_mixer` is sequence-mixing FLOPs (logged separately) and `F_wrapper` is backbone FLOPs (identical by construction). The O(L\u00b2) attention vs. O(L log L) Hyena gap is therefore **in the cost table, not in the budget adjustment** \u2014 i.e. each condition trains to its *own* per-step FLOP cost but for the same number of steps \u00d7 microbatch, so the *total* training budget in attention-FLOP equivalents is approximately equal across conditions within \u00b15%. Any residual gap is logged.\n- **Parameter count** matched (\u00b15% on the mixer module; the wrapper is shared, see \u00a75.5) with channel re-projection factor logged.\n- **Optimiser / schedule / normalisation / precision** identical (FP32, no mixed precision; CPU-only wall clock \u2014 researcher clarification #7).\n- **Sequence-mixing FLOPs reported separately** in the cost table so the O(L\u00b2) / O(L log L) asymmetry is visible. This is the explicit, no-cheating visibility rule.\n- **Per-condition RNG seeds**: the same seed initialises the wrapper weights; the mixer weights are initialised from a *deterministic* function of the seed so seed-to-condition pairing is one-to-one (this enables paired Wilcoxon over matched seeds).\n- **Data identity**: identical minibatch sequence per optimiser step across all three conditions at matched seed (same train instance), so the only varying signal is the operator.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Pre-registered headline metrics\n\nThese five metrics are pre-registered. They define what \"answer-grade evidence\" looks like for this research question.\n\n1. **Error-vs-horizon slope.** Per condition, regress `log(MAE_rollout)` on `log(L)` over `L \u2208 {128, 256, 1024, 4096, 8192, 16384}`. Headline = slope `\u03b1_C`. A smaller (less positive) `\u03b1` means flatter error growth.\n2. **PSD Jensen\u2013Shannon divergence at long-tail frequencies.** For each rollout at `L = 16384`, compute per-state PSD, partition log-frequency axis into three bands (short / mid / long-tail). Headline = `JSD_long_tail(PSD_reference \u2016 PSD_rollout)`.\n3. **Terminal-state drift / integrated error at horizon 16384.** Two values: `\u2016x_16384,rollout \u2212 x_16384,ref\u2016\u2082` (terminal drift) and `(1/16384) \u00b7 \u03a3_t \u2016x_t,rollout \u2212 x_t,ref\u2016\u2082` (time-averaged MAE).\n4. **Stability rate.** Fraction of rollouts that complete 16384 steps without producing NaN, Inf, or exceeding a per-state magnitude threshold (5 \u03c3 of the validation marginal). A flaky rollout gets a `0`.\n5. **Lyapunov exponent estimate on rollouts.** Computed via the standard finite-time / Benettin algorithm along the rollout trajectory, averaged over the ensemble. Lower (less positive) `\u03bb` on the rollout means the learned operator is reproducing the chaotic divergence structure rather than amplifying or damping it.\n\n### What constitutes a positive answer to the research question (binding, pre-registered)\n\n> **C1 produces a significantly flatter error-vs-horizon slope (metric 1) and a significantly smaller PSD-JSD at long-tail frequencies (metric 2) than C0, under matched compute and \u2265 3 seeds, AND C1 outperforms the causal-Hyena ablation (C2) on metrics 1, 2, 3 at the longest horizons.**\n\nAnything weaker (C1 better on aggregate benchmark only, C1 better only at a single horizon, C1 better only on one dataset) is **correlative, not answer-grade**, and is flagged as such in any readout. This criterion is stated explicitly to remove post-hoc equivocation.\n\n### Secondary metrics (logged, not headline)\n\n- ACF match at lags `{1, 8, 64, 512, 4096}` evaluated at horizon 16384 \u2014 completes the multi-timescale picture described in \u00a711 of the plan.\n- Per-band MAE across the three PSD bands at horizon 16384.\n- Wasserstein-1 distance between rollout-ensemble and reference marginals on `(state, derivative, energy)`.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Training and evaluation protocol\n\n### 10.1. Training\n\n- Mode: standard SiT stochastic-interpolant training. Samples `(t, x_t, x_1, c)` per step. Loss: standard interpolant denoising/consistency objective (not enumerated here because it is shared across conditions; whichever SiT loss is the published default \u2014 \u03b1/\u03b2 schedules included \u2014 is fixed and applied identically).\n- Optimiser: AdamW, learning rate `\u03b7` (one value chosen up-front, fixed), cosine schedule, linear warm-up for the first 5% of steps, weight decay applied to all matrices only (no decay on biases / norm), gradient clipping `\u2016g\u2016\u2082 \u2264 1`.\n- Total optimiser steps: `N_steps = 200,000` (pre-registered; can be revised only *before* any seed runs).\n- Microbatch: chosen to fill available memory; gradient accumulation holds the *effective batch size* fixed across conditions at `B_eff = 256` sequences \u00d7 512 tokens.\n- Evaluation cadence: every 10,000 steps on the validation set; final model is the one with the best validation metric among the saved checkpoints (the chosen metric is \"terminal-state drift at 16384 on validation\" \u2014 pre-registered).\n- Seeds: **K = 5** per condition (the floor `K = 3` applies only if compute forces it).\n\n### 10.2. Evaluation \u2014 two modes, reported separately\n\nPer researcher clarification #4, **both** rollout modes are evaluated and reported separately:\n\n- **Autoregressive rollout.** Apply the learned SiT sampler over windows of `L_train = 512`; feed the predicted last `H` tokens back as the conditioning prefix for the next window until the desired horizon is reached. This is the *long-horizon consistency* mode.\n- **One-shot interpolant sampling.** Condition on `(t_0, x_0, t_L, x_initial_distribution)` and draw the full 16384-step trajectory in one sampling pass \u2014 SiT's native mode. This is the *horizon extension without error accumulation* mode.\n\nFor each, compute metrics 1\u20135. The two modes answer different sub-questions:\n- Autoregressive curve answers \"does the operator stay stable over a forced long rollout?\"\n- One-shot curve answers \"can the operator directly produce an extremely long trajectory without compounding errors?\"\n\nThe contrast is informative: a non-causal long kernel should help *both* (long receptive field + global bidirectional context); if it helps only one, that localises the mechanism.\n\n### 10.3. Rollout ensemble\n\nPer seed, ensemble size `N_ens = 50` rollouts from held-out initial-condition distribution. 5 seeds \u00d7 50 = 250 rollouts per condition per horizon per system. Reporting unit: per-seed mean \u00b1 std, grand mean \u00b1 std across seeds, paired Wilcoxon across matched seed pairs (5 pairs), and 95% bootstrap CI (10,000 resamples) on `metric_C1 \u2212 metric_C0`.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Statistical read-out\n\n- **K = 5** random seeds per condition (`K = 3` is a documented floor, not a default).\n- Reporting stats: mean \u00b1 std across seeds (per metric per horizon per system).\n- Significance: paired **Wilcoxon signed-rank test** on matched seed pairs across the five headline metrics (1\u20135) at the longest horizon (16384), Holm-corrected across the five metrics (family-wise \u03b1 = 0.05).\n- Effect size: **95% bootstrap CI** (10,000 resamples) on `metric_C1 \u2212 metric_C0` and `metric_C1 \u2212 metric_C2`. Confidence-interval *exclusion of zero* is the \"significantly different\" criterion.\n- Pre-registration fixes (i) the metric set, (ii) the horizon set, (iii) the significance criterion, (iv) the correction method, (v) the seed-count floor, so post-hoc addition of metrics or horizons does not enter the answer-grade evidence.\n- **Mechanism attribution rule.** Per \u00a73, an improvement is decomposed:\n  - C1 \u2248 C2 > C0 \u21d2 the gain is the *long-convolutional* kernel; bidirectionality is not required.\n  - C1 > C2 \u2248 C0 \u21d2 the gain is *bidirectionality on a long kernel*; Hyena-as-such does not help without bidirectionality.\n  - C1 > C2 > C0 \u21d2 both factors contribute.\n  - C2 > C1 \u21d2 the hypothesis *non-causal Hyena is necessary* is falsified.\n- **Sensitivity ablations** (logged as supporting, not primary):\n  1. Halve the Hyena implicit-filter length (`L \u2192 L/2`, retrain C1 with `L_train = 256`).\n  2. Replace the implicit positional-filter parameterisation with a fixed Gaussian kernel of matching length and width (this isolates filter *learnability*).\n  3. \u00b15% channel-width bracketing to confirm the parameter-match doesn't drive the result.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Feasibility under the bubblewrap environment\n\n### 12.1. Verdict (binding)\n\nFull SiT+Hyena training is **not executable in the current bubblewrap**:\n\n- **CPU only**, no GPU.\n- **No network**; `pip` cannot fetch wheels.\n- `numpy`, `scipy`, `torch` are **not installed** (verified by direct import attempts).\n- Only the Python 3.12.3 stdlib plus unrelated system packages are present.\n\nThe stdlib-only micro-probe is **explicitly excluded from the answer-grade evidence** (researcher clarification #10). The deliverable here is the design, the pre-registered metrics, the analysis plan, and projected cost tables \u2014 *not* a measurement of any trained-model behaviour.\n\n### 12.2. Per-runtime cost projections (labelled as projections, not measurements)\n\nThe figures below are *order-of-magnitude projections* from the published SiT and Hyena recipes and the basic compute accounting, scaled by `L` using the asymptotic costs in \u00a75. They are projections only; **no measurement has been made**. The wall-time estimates condition on PyTorch (CPU BLAS) being available; under the current bubblewrap, the dependency prerequisite is not satisfied.\n\nPer-sequence-mixer FLOPs per token-step (analytical, condition-dependent):\n\n| Mixer | Per-token-step FLOPs | At L = 512 | At L = 1024 | At L = 4096 | At L = 8192 | At L = 16384 |\n|-------|----------------------|-----------|-------------|-------------|-------------|--------------|\n| C0 attention (per-layer, per-sample) `O(L D\u00b2)` for projections + `O(L\u00b2 D)` for QK^T | `~ 2 L D\u00b2 + L\u00b2 D` | \u2248 `2\u00b7512\u00b7768\u00b2 + 512\u00b2\u00b7768 \u2248 8.1\u00b710\u2078` (for `D=768`) | \u2248 `1.6\u00b710\u2079 + 1.0\u00b710\u2079 \u2248 2.6\u00b710\u2079` | \u2248 `6.5\u00b710\u2079 + 1.6\u00b710\u00b9\u2070 \u2248 2.2\u00b710\u00b9\u2070` | \u2248 `1.3\u00b710\u00b9\u2070 + 6.4\u00b710\u00b9\u2070 \u2248 7.7\u00b710\u00b9\u2070` | \u2248 `2.6\u00b710\u00b9\u2070 + 2.6\u00b710\u00b9\u00b9 \u2248 2.8\u00b710\u00b9\u00b9` |\n| C1 / C2 Hyena FFT (per-layer, per-sample) `O(L log L \u00b7 D)` + `O(D\u00b2)` gating + `O(D\u00b2)` MLP | `~ D\u00b2 + L log L \u00b7 D + L \u00b7 D\u00b2` | \u2248 `D\u00b2 + 512\u00b79\u00b7D + 512\u00b7D\u00b2 \u2248 7.7\u00b710\u2075` (`D=768`) | \u2248 `4\u00b710\u2076 + 1024\u00b710\u00b7768 + 512\u00b7D\u00b2 \u2248 1.1\u00b710\u2077` | \u2248 `2\u00b710\u2077 + 4096\u00b712\u00b7768 + 4096\u00b7D\u00b2 \u2248 2.5\u00b710\u2079` | \u2248 `5\u00b710\u2077 + 8192\u00b713\u00b7768 + 8192\u00b7D\u00b2 \u2248 1.0\u00b710\u00b9\u2070` | \u2248 `1\u00b710\u2078 + 16384\u00b714\u00b7768 + 16384\u00b7D\u00b2 \u2248 2.0\u00b710\u00b9\u2070` |\n\n(These rows illustrate the asymptotic gap; their absolute constants depend on the exact SiT envelope chosen in \u00a75.5 and on the Hyena implicit-filter MLP width, both of which are the engineer's logged choice. They are projections *of the cost model*, not measurements.)\n\nAsymmetry at `L = 16384`: Hyena is `O(L log L) \u2248 16384\u00b714\u00b7768 \u2248 1.8\u00b710\u2078` per layer per sample for the FFT, vs attention's `O(L\u00b2) \u2248 16384\u00b2\u00b7768 \u2248 2\u00b710\u00b9\u00b9` \u2014 i.e. the **headline cost gap is ~10\u00b3 at the longest horizon**, which is *the* reason this question is interesting. The experiment is designed to surface whether that cost gap buys the claimed consistency gain.\n\n### 12.3. Projected wall-time (CPU vs indicative GPU)\n\nConditions for the projection to be interpretable:\n- SiT envelope: depth `L_blocks`, width `D`, MLP expansion `r`. Economist's placeholder used here: SiT-B/2-class envelope (`L_blocks \u2248 12`, `D=768`, `r=4`). **Any deviation is logged; placeholders are placeholders.**\n- Per-step overhead besides mixer: 1.0\u00d7 per-sequence-mixer cost as a coarse upper bound (the wrapper itself is non-trivial but is shared across conditions, so its contribution cancels).\n- CPU: 24 vCPUs, FP32, BLAS single-thread per op, ~`1\u00b710\u00b9\u2070` FLOPs/sec sustained (typical x86_64 BLAS).\n- Indicative GPU: 1\u00d7 A100 (FP32 ~`1.9\u00b710\u00b9\u2074` FLOPs/sec peak, ~`6\u00b710\u00b9\u00b3` sustained on attention-bound TF32-equivalent workloads \u2014 but the policy is FP32, so the sustained figure used is `~3\u00b710\u00b9\u00b3`).\n\nProjected per-condition wall-time at 5 seeds \u00d7 200,000 steps \u00d7 `B_eff = 256` \u00d7 `L_train = 512`:\n\n| Condition | Per-step mixer FLOPs (B_eff\u00b7L\u00b7[mixer-per-token]) | Per-step mixer FLOPs (projected) | CPU wall-time (5 seeds, projected) | 1\u00d7 A100 wall-time (5 seeds, projected) |\n|-----------|---------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| C0 (attention) | `256 \u00b7 512 \u00b7 8.1\u00b710\u2078 \u2248 1.06\u00b710\u00b9\u2074` | \u2248 `1.1\u00b710\u00b9\u2074` | \u2248 `5\u00b710\u2074` sec-mix \u00d7 `T_factor` \u2248 days (rough) | \u2248 `5\u00b710\u2074 / (6\u00b710\u00b3) = ~10` seconds-mix-equivalent \u00d7 seed count \u2192 hours total |\n| C1 / C2 (Hyena) | `256 \u00b7 512 \u00b7 7.7\u00b710\u2075 \u2248 1.0\u00b710\u00b9\u00b9` | \u2248 `1.0\u00b710\u00b9\u00b9` | \u2248 `~50\u00d7 faster` than C0 on the mixer | \u2248 `~50\u00d7 faster` than C0 on the mixer |\n\nThese numbers are deliberately rough. They are **projections**, not the deliverable, and they are reported only to satisfy the researcher's request for a per-runtime cost table. They are not evidence in support of any claim about the answer \u2014 they are evidence about whether the experiment could run at all.\n\n### 12.4. Contingency plan (if execution environment becomes available later)\n\nIf and only if a non-bubblewrap environment (GPU with PyTorch, NumPy, SciPy installed; network access for downloads; \u2265 100 GB free disk for checkpoints) becomes available, the reduced-surrogate fallback is:\n\n- **Same three conditions (C0, C1, C2), same SiT wrapper, same metric set, same statistics.**\n- **Reduced SiT envelope.** Drop to SiT-Ti or SiT-S class (`L_blocks = 6`, `D = 384`, narrow MLP) so 5-seed training fits in the available compute. **Document the scale-down**; pre-registered metrics and statistics are unchanged. The sign of the answer is then interpretable; the magnitude is not directly comparable to full-scale.\n- **Single-system subset** if even the reduced envelope is too heavy: Lorenz-96 (`N = 40`, `F = 8`) only, with all four systems logged as \"not run due to compute\". Pre-registered horizons retained; do not truncate them.\n- **Long horizon reduction only as last resort.** If 16384 is unreachable, fall back to maximum reachable `L_max \u2260 16384` and report it as a documented limitation; the pre-registered `L = 16384` headline is then retracted and the longest-reachable horizon becomes supplementary, not headline, evidence.\n\nThis fallback is itself a design choice. It is *not* an executed run and produces *no* fabricated numbers in this deliverable.\n\n---\n\n## 13. What is answer-grade vs correlative (explicit adjudication rule)\n\nRe-stated for clarity; binds all reporting.\n\n- **Answer-grade.** C1 < C0 on metric 1 (flatter slope) AND C1 < C0 on metric 2 (smaller PSD-JSD at long-tail) with both CIs excluding zero at \u03b1 = 0.05 across at least one of the four dynamical systems, AND C1 < C2 on metrics 1, 2, 3 at the longest horizon on the same system. This is the pre-registered answer definition.\n- **Correlative / insufficient.** Any of: improvement only on a single horizon, only on a single metric, only on aggregate benchmark, only under a single seed pairing, or driven by the parameter-count gap rather than the operator. The cause is then investigated before any claim is made.\n- **Falsifying.** C2 \u2265 C1 on the headline metrics at the longest horizon \u2192 \"non-causality is necessary\" is falsified. Reported as a falsification, not softened.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Deliverable boundary (explicit contract)\n\nThis submission is a **design-only** deliverable.\n\n- No experiment has been run in the current environment. (Confirmed impossible: bubblewrap is CPU-only, no numpy/scipy/torch, no network.)\n- No numerical outcome has been fabricated. All numerical values present are projections of cost models from published SiT/Hyena recipes; the answers to \u00a79 are *criteria*, not measurements.\n- No source code outside pure-Python stdlib is imported or executed by this design.\n- The repository (`/workspace`) contains only `task.json` (and no SiT/Hyena source), and the design is self-contained against canonical published descriptions of SiT and Hyena.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Summary checklist (what the final design commits to)\n\n- [x] Three conditions: C0 attention, C1 non-causal Hyena (primary contrast), C2 causal Hyena (mechanism-isolating ablation).\n- [x] Time-attention specified as the sequence/temporal-axis mixer; cross-attention to time embedding identical across conditions.\n- [x] Hyena operator specified: depthwise long convolution + implicit positional filter + elementwise gating + MLP; bidirectional in C1, causal in C2; FFT length, kernel parameterisation, bidirectionality rule, gating form all logged.\n- [x] Param match within \u00b15% on mixer; channel re-projection factor logged; total-mixer-only count.\n- [x] Matched compute budget with sequence-mixing FLOPs reported separately.\n- [x] Four multi-timescale dynamical systems; reference ground truth via stiff-aware integrator; fixed horizon set {128, 256, 1024, 4096, 8192, 16384}; training snippet L_train = 512.\n- [x] Identical SiT wrapper, identical optimiser/schedule/normalisation/precision; FP32; same data pipeline.\n- [x] Both rollout modes (autoregressive and one-shot interpolant sampling) evaluated and reported separately.\n- [x] Five pre-registered headline metrics with their statistical threshold rule and mechanism-attribution rule.\n- [x] Statistical read-out: K=5 seeds, paired Wilcoxon (Holm-corrected), 95% bootstrap CI on C1\u2212C0 and C1\u2212C2.\n- [x] Explicit positive-answer criterion and falsification rules.\n- [x] Feasibility verdict (not executable here) + projected cost table + contingency plan. Projections clearly labelled as projections.\n- [x] No experiments run; no fabricated outcomes.", "evidence": "Repository inspection (`/workspace/ls`, `find -maxdepth 3`) shows only `task.json`; that file's own body restates the question, context, and the contract \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\". No SiT source, no Hyena source, no configs/READMEs/notes. Runtime checks: Python 3.12.3 (x86_64); 24 vCPUs via both `nproc` and `multiprocessing.cpu_count()`; ~688 GB free disk; `numpy`, `scipy`, `torch` not importable; `pip` is offline (bubblewrap, no network). The cost table in \u00a712.2 and \u00a712.3 is presented explicitly as projections of cost models from published SiT (Ma et al., NeurIPS 2024) and Hyena (Poli et al., ICML 2023) recipes, not as measurements. The researcher clarification message supplies the binding constraints (operator swap specificity, C2 mandatory, both rollout modes, the four dynamical systems, the horizon set, parameter/precision matching, the five headline metrics, the positive-answer criterion, the K=5 seed count, the feasibility verdict, and the exclusion of a stdlib micro-probe from answer-grade evidence). The Lyapanov algorithm, PSD decomposition, paired Wilcoxon + Holm correction, and 95% bootstrap-CI effect size are standard per-system reporting choices already used in the field.", "assumptions": ["SiT envelope refers to the \"SiT-B/2-class\"-type placeholder (depth \u2248 12, width 768, MLP expansion 4). Any deviation is logged in `operators.yaml`; the placeholders are placeholders, not claims, and \u00a712.3 carries that caveat inside the table caption.", "The \"wrapper\" parameters (depth, MLP, norm placement, residual coefficients, optimiser, schedule, gradient clipping, FP32) are fixed and are not part of the ablation.", "Channel re-projection `D' = round(D \u00b7 \u03c1)` with `\u03c1 \u2208 [0.95, 1.00]` keeps parameter match to \u00b15%; values that cannot reach \u00b15% are logged with a sensitivity sweep (sensitivity ablation #3) to ensure they do not drive the headline outcome.", "Bidirectional Hyena in C1 is implemented as a single real-valued FFT of length `2L` against the padded sequence, OR two parallel causal filters `h_fwd, h_bwd` summed output-wise \u2014 both specifications are considered equivalent for the purposes of this design; the implementation chosen in any eventual code is logged so it is reproducible.", "Implicit positional-filter parameterisation: a small MLP maps positional indices to complex/real filter coefficients; this is the standard Hyena recipe and is held fixed across C1 and C2 unless the sensitivity ablation (#2) is invoked.", "Dataset split: \u2265 1M token-steps training; 50 trajectories \u00d7 16384 validation; 50 \u00d7 16384 test. Stony-number choices are placeholders; they are commitments but do not predetermine outcomes.", "Wall-time projections in \u00a712.3 condition on Python scientific libraries being available; the bubblewrap does not satisfy that prerequisite, which is why \u00a712.1's verdict is the binding feasibility statement.", "Pre-registered metrics, horizons, statistical threshold rule, seed-count floor (K=3) and ceiling (K=5) are committed before any seed is run. Adding or removing a metric post-hoc forfeits answer-grade status."], "uncertainties": ["Exact SiT training-loss form and \u03b1/\u03b2 schedule: assumed to be the published SiT default and applied identically across conditions; this is a commitment but not a deep design parameter because the loss is shared.", "Whether a real-coded FFT or two-filter sum implementation of \"bidirectional Hyena\" is chosen at implementation time \u2014 both are permitted; either choice is logged and identical across all C1 seeds.", "Whether the integrator outputs are stored as `dt_ref = 1/256` and downsampled or stored directly at `dt = 1` is an implementation choice; the explicit log requirement is that the *same* scheme is used across all conditions.", "If a non-bubblewrap environment becomes available, the surrogate fallback (\u00a712.4) is offered with reduction choices that include shrinking the envelope to SiT-S/Ti and possibly restricting to Lorenz-96 only; both are reductions that should be flagged in any subsequent report."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-9f6d1ba2-d3ba-4d14-b2db-9c0eb5c77758", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 19}
